hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2779","Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, narrated to us Khalid, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Tufayl who said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: ""What do you think about riding between Safa and Marwah?"" He said: ""Your people claim that it is a Sunnah."" He said: ""They spoke truthfully and falsely. The Prophet (ﷺ) came to Makkah and began to walk between Safa and Marwah. The people of Makkah came out until even the women came out. He (ﷺ) did not strike anyone near him, nor did they leave him alone. So he (ﷺ) called for his mount and rode it. Had he been left alone, he would have preferred walking.""

Abu Bakr said: The statement of Ibn 'Abbas, ""They spoke truthfully and falsely,"" means that they spoke truthfully in that the Prophet (ﷺ) did indeed ride between them, and they spoke falsely in that it is not an obligatory Sunnah nor a virtuous act. Rather, it is merely permissible, not mandatory nor virtuous.

---

**CHAPTER:**"
